WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.136
Before anything else, picture this.

00:00:02.536 --> 00:00:10.675
An aircraft software reviewer asks: prove every condition in this if-statement really matters. That proof is MC/DC.

00:00:11.875 --> 00:00:12.804
Pause and think.

00:00:13.204 --> 00:00:18.126
For a decision with three conditions, roughly how many tests does MC/DC need?

00:00:18.526 --> 00:00:25.841
Hold your answer; we will check it at the end. And whenever a new word comes up, we stop for a moment and explain it.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:37.310
This video covers two avionics topics from the Raytheon and Garmin roles: MC/DC test coverage, and the MIL-STD-1553 data bus.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:52.423
New word: M C/D C. Modified Condition/Decision Coverage: testing proves each condition in a decision independently changes the outcome. Picture it like this: Checking each light switch really controls the light on its own.

00:00:52.823 --> 00:01:00.498
New word: D C. Data Centre; also Direct Current. Picture it like this: A warehouse full of servers.

00:01:02.897 --> 00:01:06.623
Imagine proving each switch in a room really controls the light.

00:01:07.023 --> 00:01:08.753
Hold every other switch still.

00:01:09.153 --> 00:01:10.303
Flip just one.

00:01:10.703 --> 00:01:12.177
The bulb must change.

00:01:12.577 --> 00:01:16.954
Repeat for every switch. That's modified condition, decision coverage.

00:01:18.154 --> 00:01:20.883
Take the decision, A and B, or C.

00:01:21.283 --> 00:01:28.295
For A, compare true true false, which is true, with false true false, which is false. Only A changed.

00:01:28.695 --> 00:01:32.306
For B, compare true true false with true false false.

00:01:32.706 --> 00:01:38.081
For C, compare true false false, which is false, with true false true, which is true.

00:01:38.481 --> 00:01:41.674
So four tests cover three conditions: n plus one.

00:01:42.074 --> 00:01:47.844
DO-178C requires this for level A software, where failure could be catastrophic.

00:01:47.997 --> 00:01:58.365
New word: D O-178C. The standard for safety-critical airborne software. Picture it like this: The aviation rulebook for code.

00:02:00.765 --> 00:02:08.265
The MIL-STD-1553 bus uses sixteen bit words, each sent with a sync pattern and a parity bit.

00:02:08.665 --> 00:02:12.252
Five bits pick the remote terminal, one of thirty one addresses.

00:02:12.652 --> 00:02:15.392
One bit says transmit or receive.

00:02:15.792 --> 00:02:20.843
Five bits are the subaddress. Zero or thirty one means a mode code instead of data.

00:02:21.243 --> 00:02:24.610
Five bits are the word count, where zero means thirty two.

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:26.332
Recap.

00:02:26.732 --> 00:02:30.854
MC/DC shows each condition independently changes the outcome.

00:02:31.254 --> 00:02:35.619
Usually n plus one tests suffice, but masking can block pairs.

00:02:36.019 --> 00:02:41.441
A 1553 command word is five, one, five and five bits.

00:02:41.841 --> 00:02:44.592
Message schedules must fit each minor frame.

00:02:44.992 --> 00:02:50.194
Practice in lessons forty one point two and forty one point three, and problem p one ten.

00:02:51.406 --> 00:02:52.660
Back to the question.

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:57.983
For a decision with three conditions, roughly how many tests does MC/DC need?

00:02:58.383 --> 00:03:00.321
About four, n plus one.

00:03:01.521 --> 00:03:02.392
Quick recap.

00:03:02.792 --> 00:03:05.404
Each condition must flip the result on its own.

00:03:05.804 --> 00:03:09.322
1553 is a command-response bus.

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:12.810
Bus controller talks, remote terminals answer.
